Unix utility 'umount'

The Unix utility 'umount' unmounts a volume mounted earlier by 'mount', given the directory
it was mounted at. Only the root user can perform unmounts, and the root volume (/) is never
unmounted, since the whole file system hierarchy hangs from it.

With the -a parameter, every volume currently mounted is unmounted, in the reverse of the
order 'mount -a' mounted them in, so a volume mounted on top of another leaves first.

USAGE:

umount <mount point>
umount -a

Example of use:

umount /mnt

It unmounts whatever volume is mounted at /mnt.

For more information, enter 'umount ?' or 'umount --help'.

